-- Q755: IT Infrastructure and Architecture [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Each microservice shares a single database, causing payment failures to corrupt catalog and authentication data","Each microservice runs independently with isolated failure boundaries, preventing one service''s crash from cascading to others","Microservices cannot communicate with each other, which is why unrelated services remain unaffected during a crash","A monolithic architecture handles crashes better because shared memory allows all components to recover simultaneously"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under NIST SP 800-204 Section 2, microservices architecture decomposes applications into independently deployable services with isolated failure boundaries. Each service runs in its own process, so one service crashing does not cascade to others — unlike a monolith where a single failure can bring down the entire application.
Wrong (A): Microservices typically own their own data stores rather than sharing a single database, so a payment service crash would not corrupt other services'' data.
Wrong (C): Microservices do communicate via APIs and message queues; isolation comes from independent processes, not lack of communication.
Wrong (D): Monolithic architectures are more vulnerable to cascading failures because all components share a single process and memory space.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 755;

-- Q758: IT Infrastructure and Architecture [hard/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Provisioning infrastructure through machine-readable configuration files rather than manual processes","Documenting server configurations in version-controlled spreadsheets for team reference","Writing custom firmware to automate network hardware deployment and updates","Developing applications optimized to run directly on bare-metal server hardware"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under NIST SP 800-128 Section 2, Infrastructure as Code (IaC) manages and provisions computing infrastructure through machine-readable definition files such as Terraform or CloudFormation templates. This enables version control, repeatability, and automated deployments while treating infrastructure as auditable software artifacts.
Wrong (B): Spreadsheets are static documentation tools that lack automated provisioning capability and cannot enforce configuration consistency across environments.
Wrong (C): Custom firmware development targets hardware-level programming, not the automated provisioning and configuration of cloud infrastructure resources.
Wrong (D): Bare-metal application development refers to software optimization, not the infrastructure provisioning and configuration management that IaC addresses.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 758;

-- Q760: ERP and Accounting Information Systems [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A standalone accounting application that manages general ledger entries and financial reporting","A point-of-sale system designed to process and record daily retail transactions","A spreadsheet-based consolidation tool for aggregating departmental financial data","An integrated suite of modules sharing a common database across all business functions"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under COBIT 2019 Section BAI03, an ERP system integrates multiple business functions — finance, HR, procurement, production, and sales — into a single platform with a shared database. This eliminates data silos and enables real-time cross-departmental visibility and consistency.
Wrong (A): A standalone general ledger application handles only financial transactions without providing cross-functional integration for HR, procurement, or production.
Wrong (B): A point-of-sale system is limited to retail transaction processing and does not unify back-office functions like HR or inventory management.
Wrong (C): Spreadsheet-based consolidation is a manual process that cannot provide the real-time integration, shared database, or workflow automation that defines an ERP system.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 760;

-- Q772: ERP and Accounting Information Systems [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Role-based access controls with a documented segregation matrix showing AP entry and payment approval assigned to different users","A company policy memo directing that employees should not approve their own payment requests without system enforcement","Super-user accounts shared among the accounting team with verbal agreements to maintain duty segregation","An ERP configuration that logs all transactions but permits any authenticated user to perform any function"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): SOX Section 404 requires management to assess and report on internal controls over financial reporting. Role-based access controls with a documented SoD matrix provide system-enforced preventive controls that auditors can directly test, offering the strongest evidence of segregation.
Wrong (B): A policy memo establishes management intent but lacks system enforcement — users could still violate the policy without automated detection or prevention.
Wrong (C): Shared super-user accounts eliminate individual accountability and make it impossible to verify that segregation of duties is actually maintained.
Wrong (D): Logging all transactions is a detective control only; without restricting access, it cannot prevent unauthorized users from performing incompatible functions.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 772;

-- Q773: ERP and Accounting Information Systems [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Manually recalculate depreciation for all 4,200 assets to ensure complete and thorough audit coverage","Review ERP depreciation settings against accounting policy, then test a stratified sample of both asset types","Accept the ERP output without testing because automated system calculations are inherently reliable","Test only equipment assets acquired in the current year since those are most likely to contain errors"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 315, the auditor should understand automated controls by reviewing ERP configuration settings against the accounting policy, then substantively test a stratified sample of both asset types. This combines configuration verification with targeted testing for reasonable assurance.
Wrong (A): Manually recalculating all 4,200 assets is impractical and unnecessary when configuration review plus stratified sampling provides sufficient audit evidence.
Wrong (C): Accepting automated output without testing violates auditing standards — system configurations may be incorrect, providing no assurance over calculation accuracy.
Wrong (D): Testing only current-year equipment ignores buildings and prior-year assets, which may have misconfigured useful lives or salvage values.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 773;

-- Q774: Data Management and Lifecycle [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A framework of policies, roles, and standards ensuring data quality, security, and compliance enterprise-wide","A technical process for performing automated nightly database backup and recovery operations","A physical security program protecting data center facilities from unauthorized access and damage","An encryption program that protects all organizational data at rest across storage systems"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under COBIT 2019 Section APO14 (Managed Data), data governance is the enterprise-wide framework of policies, roles, and standards that ensures data quality, security, and compliance throughout the data lifecycle. It assigns accountability through data owners, stewards, and custodians.
Wrong (B): Database backup is a narrow technical operation focused on data recovery, not the broad policy-driven management framework that data governance encompasses.
Wrong (C): Physical security protects the data center facility but does not address data quality, classification, or lifecycle management policies.
Wrong (D): Encryption protects data confidentiality at rest but does not address the organizational roles, standards, and quality controls that define data governance.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 774;

-- Q777: Data Management and Lifecycle [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A primary key constraint enforcing unique row identification within the table","A referential integrity constraint linking records to valid entries in another table","A check constraint validating that column values fall within a defined range","A unique constraint preventing duplicate values in a non-key column"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under COBIT 2019 Section DSS06 (Managed Business Process Controls), referential integrity enforced through foreign key constraints ensures that a value in one table must match a valid record in the referenced table. This prevents orphaned records and maintains cross-table data consistency.
Wrong (A): A primary key constraint uniquely identifies each record within its own table but does not enforce relationships between separate tables.
Wrong (C): A check constraint validates that values meet specific conditions within a single column but does not verify relationships across tables.
Wrong (D): A unique constraint prevents duplicate values in a column but does not require that values reference existing records in another table.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 777;

-- Q780: Data Management and Lifecycle [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Change data capture or temporal tables recording all field-level modifications with timestamps","Database indexing structures that optimize query performance for frequently accessed records","Table partitioning strategies that distribute data across storage volumes by date range","Database normalization processes that eliminate redundancy by decomposing tables into related structures"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under COBIT 2019 Section DSS06.03, change data capture and temporal tables record every modification to database records, preserving before-and-after values with timestamps and user identification. This creates an immutable audit trail supporting accountability and regulatory compliance.
Wrong (B): Database indexing creates ordered lookup structures to improve query speed but does not record or track data modifications over time.
Wrong (C): Table partitioning distributes data across storage segments for performance optimization but does not capture historical changes to individual records.
Wrong (D): Database normalization eliminates data redundancy through table decomposition but provides no mechanism for tracking who changed a value or when.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 780;

-- Q785: Data Management and Lifecycle [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["It converts the relational database engine into a NoSQL document-oriented storage format","It eliminates the need for database indexes by organizing data into self-contained segments","It enables the engine to scan only relevant partitions, improving performance for date-filtered queries","It automatically removes all historical data that falls outside the boundaries of active partitions"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under COBIT 2019 Section DSS05, table partitioning by date range enables partition pruning, where the database engine scans only partitions matching the query''s date filter rather than the entire table. This dramatically reduces I/O and execution time for date-specific queries.
Wrong (A): Partitioning organizes data within a relational database — it does not convert the database engine to NoSQL or change the data model.
Wrong (B): Partitioning complements indexing rather than replacing it; indexes are still needed within each partition for optimal query performance.
Wrong (D): Partitioning segments data logically but does not automatically delete historical records — data retention policies are managed separately.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 785;

-- Q792: System Availability and Change Management [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["An executive-authorized change that receives priority scheduling in the next maintenance window","A routine patch applied during a pre-approved and regularly scheduled maintenance window","A change implemented immediately to address a critical issue, bypassing normal approval processes","A planned upgrade whose implementation timeline has been accelerated to an earlier date"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ITIL 4 Section 5.2 (Change Enablement), an emergency change addresses a critical issue — such as a security breach or active exploitation — that requires immediate implementation. It bypasses normal CAB review but requires post-implementation documentation and review.
Wrong (A): Executive authorization alone does not define an emergency change; the defining characteristic is the critical nature of the issue requiring immediate action.
Wrong (B): A routine patch applied during a scheduled maintenance window is a standard or normal change, not an emergency change requiring immediate deployment.
Wrong (D): Accelerating a planned upgrade''s timeline is a schedule change, not an emergency change — the upgrade was already approved through normal processes.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 792;

-- Q795: System Availability and Change Management [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["This percentage is within normal operating parameters and requires no additional audit investigation","The high rate suggests the change process may be circumvented by classifying routine changes as emergencies","Emergency changes should be prohibited entirely to ensure all changes follow standard approval procedures","The auditor should focus review efforts exclusively on non-emergency changes as those carry greater risk"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ITIL 4 Section 5.2 and COBIT 2019 Section BAI06, emergency changes should represent a small fraction of total changes — industry benchmarks typically range from 5–10%. A 15% rate suggests potential process circumvention where staff classify routine changes as emergencies to bypass standard approval controls.
Wrong (A): A 15% emergency rate exceeds the industry benchmark of 5–10% and is a red flag warranting investigation into whether changes truly met emergency criteria.
Wrong (C): Eliminating emergency changes entirely is impractical — legitimate emergencies like active security exploits require an expedited approval path.
Wrong (D): Emergency changes actually carry higher risk due to reduced oversight, making them a priority for audit review rather than exclusion.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 795;

-- Q798: System Availability and Change Management [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["To serve as a secure vault for storing and managing user authentication credentials","To maintain a comprehensive record of IT assets, their configurations, and interdependencies","To function as a centralized system for tracking employee attendance and scheduling","To operate as a transaction processing engine for recording and settling financial operations"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ITIL 4 Section 5.2 (Configuration Management), a CMDB maintains a comprehensive inventory of IT assets (configuration items), their attributes, and the relationships between them. This supports change management, incident management, and impact analysis.
Wrong (A): Password management systems handle authentication credentials, which is a security function separate from the asset and configuration tracking purpose of a CMDB.
Wrong (C): Employee attendance tracking is an HR function unrelated to IT asset configuration management and infrastructure dependency mapping.
Wrong (D): Financial transaction processing is handled by ERP or accounting systems, not a CMDB designed for IT infrastructure visibility.$EXPL$
